---
title: "Get started with alphaN in Stata"
description: >
  Install the command, calibrate a significance level, and turn regression
  output into Bayes factors.
---

```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("alphaN.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

`alphan` is the Stata version of the R package `alphaN`. It sets the
significance level of a regression coefficient as a decreasing function of the
sample size, so that a significant result corresponds to at least a chosen
Bayes factor in favor of the alternative hypothesis. This page goes through
every subcommand. The same steps in R are in
[Get started in R](intro-alphaN.html).

## Installation

<div class="wrap-code">

```stata
net install alphan, from("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jespernwulff/alphaN-stata/main/")
```

</div>

The command is written for Stata 14 or later and has been tested on StataNow
19.5. Nothing else needs to be installed. To
update an existing installation, add the `replace` option. After installation,
`help alphan` opens the help file, which is also
[on this site](stata-help.html).

## Calibrating the significance level

We are planning a regression with 1,000 observations. By default, `alphan`
finds the level at which rejecting the null hypothesis means the null and the
alternative are at least equally likely, a Bayes factor of 1, which rules out
Lindley's paradox.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "first")
```

Raising the target asks for more evidence before you are allowed to reject,
which lowers the level. A Bayes factor of 3 is conventionally called moderate
evidence and 10 strong evidence. The `bf()` option takes a list.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "targets")
```

Sample sizes are also a list, and the level falls as they grow.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "grows")
```

The result is left in `r(alpha)`, and the whole table in `r(table)`, for use
in the rest of a do-file.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "stored")
```

## Choosing the prior

The `method()` option selects the prior behind the Bayes factor. `jab`, the
default, `min`, `robust` and `balanced` invert Jeffreys' approximate Bayes
factor with different choices of the prior. For instance, `balanced` balances
the Type I and Type II errors, assuming realistic effects are uniform between
0 and `upper()`.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "balanced")
```

The methods `es` and `moment` calibrate to the effect-size and moment Bayes
factors of Klauer, Meyer-Grant and Kellen (2025), whose priors center the
alternative on an effect size you choose with `de()`. Because the moment prior
assigns zero density to the null value itself and little to effects near it,
the level it implies falls much faster with the sample size.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "klauer")
```

For a regression, give `p()`, the number of other estimated coefficients
including the intercept, to obtain the calibration that is exact for the
normal linear model. [Choosing a calibration](choosing.html) discusses how to
pick a method and a target.

Joint *F* tests of several coefficients take `q()`, the number of coefficients
tested. The targeted effect is then on Cohen's *f* scale, so `de(sqrt(.15))`
targets a medium effect.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "joint")
```

## From regression output to Bayes factors

`alphan jab`, typed after an estimation command, reports Jeffreys' approximate
Bayes factor for every coefficient except the intercept.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "regress")
```

The last column is the Bayes factor in favor of the alternative. `mpg` is far
from significant, and the Bayes factor of 0.12 says more: the data are about
eight times as likely under the null hypothesis. Name a coefficient to report
only that one, and choose another prior with `method()`.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "jab-one")
```

The command works after any estimator that stores `e(b)`, `e(V)` and `e(N)`.
It uses the standard errors of the fitted model, so a `vce()` option carries
over.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "logit")
```

If all you have is a result reported in a paper, convert the statistic or the
*p*-value. With `df()`, `alphan jabp` reads the *p*-value as coming from a *t*
test; without it, from a *z* test.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "reported")
```

`alphan klauerbf` does the same for the effect-size and moment Bayes factors.
Here is a *t* statistic of 2.24 on 79 degrees of freedom, against an
alternative centered on a medium effect.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "klauerbf")
```

## Power at the calibrated level

A lower level costs power. `alphan power` gives the power of the coefficient
test at the calibrated level against a standardized effect `d()`, the quantity
that the *t* statistic divided by the square root of the sample size estimates.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "power")
```

## A report for your preregistration

`alphan report` records every input, the resulting level, the decision rule,
the power against the effects you care about and the references to cite. Add
`saving(report.md, replace)` to write it to a file.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "report")
```

## Graphs

`alphan plot` compares the level across sample sizes. Other `twoway` options
are passed through, and the data in memory are left alone.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "plot", output = FALSE)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, out.width = "100%", fig.alt = "The significance level for a Bayes factor of 3 against the sample size, for six calibration methods."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("gs-plot.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

`alphan powerplot` shows what each calibration costs in power, with every
method at its own level and a fixed .05 as the dashed reference.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "powerplot", output = FALSE)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, out.width = "100%", fig.alt = "Power against the sample size for effects of 0.1 and 0.5, for six calibration methods and a fixed level of .05."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("gs-powerplot.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

`alphan jabplot` shows how the Bayes factor maps onto the *p*-value at a given
sample size. The triangle marks the level for your target; the dotted lines
mark the levels for Bayes factors of 1, 3 and 10.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("getting-started", "jabplot", output = FALSE)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, out.width = "100%", fig.alt = "The Bayes factor against the p-value at a sample size of 1,000."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("gs-jabplot.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

